MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

C++ constructors observe `this` (FLTK widgets self-register children
holding back-pointers), so Rust's construct-then-bitwise-move protocol
left dangling self-references. Three cooperating MIR rewrites remove
every temporary on the construction path:

- shape 1 (CxxCtorInplace, post-inline; registered both after
  RemoveUnneededDrops and after SimplifyLocals::Final): collapses
  `tmp = call ctor(); place = (move|copy) tmp` into a direct call
  destination when tmp is a single-def/single-use local and the final
  place is rooted at _0 or at a Deref of a stable pointer local —
  this folds `p.write(D::new(..))` into `(*p) = call D::new(..)`.

- shape 2 (CxxCtorInplaceEarly, pre-inline): rewrites the importer's
  by-value binding `new` (MaybeUninit slot + as_mut_ptr + extern ctor
  + assume_init) so the C++ constructor writes the sret return slot
  directly (`&raw mut _0`). Gated on #[repr(cpp)] or
  #[rustc_cxx_imported_vtable] return types; structure re-verified
  (use counts, MaybeUninit<T> == ret).

- shape 3 (shape3_deaggregate, pre-inline): a #[constructor] body's
  `tmp = call Base::new(); _0 = Self { __base: move tmp, .. }`
  becomes `(_0.__base) = call Base::new(..)` plus per-field stores;
  tolerant of ElaborateDrops drop-flag interposition.

End to end the chain `ed.write(RustEditor::new(..))` →
`Self { __base: Fl_Text_Editor::new(..) }` → extern C++ ctor now
constructs at the final heap address: the advanced-editor self-test's
children-parent-pointer probe flips from DANGLED to VALID with no
re-parent fix-up.
